Input: {"question": "What is the typical color of the interior surface of the fruiting bodies of the Fairy Cup?", "original_source_segments": {"D1S1": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0287-4125c5beba7f:d00", "start": 0, "end": 164, "text": "Peziza praetervisa Peziza praetervisa, commonly known as the purple fairy cup or the fireplace cup, is a species of fungus in the genus \"Peziza\", family Pezizaceae."}, "D1S2": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0287-4125c5beba7f:d00", "start": 165, "end": 304, "text": "Recognized by its flattened, purple, cup-like fruitbodies, this widespread fungus typically grows scattered or in clusters on burnt ground."}, "D1S3": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0287-4125c5beba7f:d00", "start": 305, "end": 380, "text": "The fungus was first described scientifically in 1897 by Giacomo Bresadola."}, "D1S4": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0287-4125c5beba7f:d00", "start": 381, "end": 442, "text": "Fruitbodies resemble flattened cups with diameters of up to ."}, "D1S5": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0287-4125c5beba7f:d00", "start": 443, "end": 546, "text": "The inner spore-bearing surface, the hymenium, is purple to purple-brown, but lightens to brown in age."}, "D1S6": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0287-4125c5beba7f:d00", "start": 547, "end": 649, "text": "The exterior surface is pale purple and scurfy (covered with small flakes or scales that are shed from"}, "D2S1": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0287-4125c5beba7f:d01", "start": 0, "end": 145, "text": "Peziza praetervisa, commonly known as the purple fairy cup or the fireplace cup, is a species of fungus in the genus \"Peziza\", family Pezizaceae."}, "D2S2": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0287-4125c5beba7f:d01", "start": 146, "end": 285, "text": "Recognized by its flattened, purple, cup-like fruitbodies, this widespread fungus typically grows scattered or in clusters on burnt ground."}, "D2S3": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0287-4125c5beba7f:d01", "start": 286, "end": 361, "text": "The fungus was first described scientifically in 1897 by Giacomo Bresadola."}, "D2S4": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0287-4125c5beba7f:d01", "start": 362, "end": 427, "text": "Fruitbodies resemble flattened cups with diameters of up to 2 cm."}, "D2S5": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0287-4125c5beba7f:d01", "start": 428, "end": 549, "text": "The inner spore-bearing surface, the hymenium, is green to yellow-green, but lightens to a lighter shade of green in age."}, "D2S6": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0287-4125c5beba7f:d01", "start": 550, "end": 666, "text": "The exterior surface is pale purple and scurfy (covered with small flakes or scales that are shed from the surface)."}, "D3S1": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0287-4125c5beba7f:d02", "start": 0, "end": 168, "text": "Peziza violacea Peziza violacea, commonly known as the violet fairy cup or the violet cup fungus, is a species of fungus in the genus \"Peziza\" of the family Pezizaceae."}, "D3S2": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0287-4125c5beba7f:d02", "start": 169, "end": 297, "text": "As both it common names and specific epithet suggest, the cup-shaped fruiting bodies are violet colored on the interior surface."}, "D3S3": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0287-4125c5beba7f:d02", "start": 298, "end": 301, "text": "\"P."}, "D3S4": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0287-4125c5beba7f:d02", "start": 302, "end": 353, "text": "violacea\" is typically found growing on burnt soil."}, "D3S5": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0287-4125c5beba7f:d02", "start": 354, "end": 469, "text": "Fruiting bodies are initially almost spherical, then cup-shaped, then expanding to being somewhat flattened in age."}, "D3S6": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0287-4125c5beba7f:d02", "start": 470, "end": 591, "text": "They do not have a stem (or at most a short, narrowed version), and may be up to wide by 1–1.5 cm tall, although they are"}, "D4S1": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0287-4125c5beba7f:d03", "start": 0, "end": 136, "text": "sessile , about 2–6 cm ( 0.79–2.36 inches ) in diameter , with erect asci lining the inner of their cavity , called the hymenium ( Fig ."}, "D4S2": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0287-4125c5beba7f:d03", "start": 137, "end": 142, "text": "3 ) ."}, "D4S3": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0287-4125c5beba7f:d03", "start": 143, "end": 262, "text": "Between asci are sterile hyphae called paraphyses , while the rest of the fruiting body consists of supporting tissue ."}, "D4S4": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0287-4125c5beba7f:d03", "start": 263, "end": 335, "text": "After dispersion , each ascospore germinates into a new mycelium [ 1 ] ."}, "D4S5": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0287-4125c5beba7f:d03", "start": 336, "end": 341, "text": "Fig ."}, "D4S6": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0287-4125c5beba7f:d03", "start": 342, "end": 345, "text": "3 ."}, "D4S7": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0287-4125c5beba7f:d03", "start": 346, "end": 357, "text": "Peziza sp ."}, "D4S8": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0287-4125c5beba7f:d03", "start": 358, "end": 445, "text": "ascocarp morphology ( Image source : Debivort , Wikimedia ) Where Peziza can be found ?"}, "D4S9": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0287-4125c5beba7f:d03", "start": 446, "end": 525, "text": "Peziza species are commonly distributed in nature worldwide , while the species"}, "D5S1": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0287-4125c5beba7f:d04", "start": 0, "end": 132, "text": "are ecologically significant due to their saprophytic nature , which boosts soil fertility and promotes continuing nutrient cycles ."}, "D5S2": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0287-4125c5beba7f:d04", "start": 133, "end": 556, "text": "Join BYJU 'S Learning Program Send OTP Grade/E